SEC = [
 (157, 'Let us do these activities before we read', [
  'I. Uses of trees: give shade, oxygen and fresh air; fruits and food; wood for furniture and fuel; medicines (bark, leaves); home for birds and animals; bring rain; stop soil erosion; paper, gum, rubber.',
  'II. Column A (What do you know?): neem leaves are bitter; twigs clean teeth; leaves keep insects away; neem gives cool shade; Ugadi pachadi uses neem flowers. | Column B (What do you want to know?): How old can a neem tree live? Which medicines are made from it? Where else does neem grow? Why is it bitter?']),
 (159, 'Let us discuss (p. 151)', [
  '1. Neem flowers are used in Ugadi pachadi; kajal made from neem flowers cures itching in the eyes.',
  '2. Neem cleans teeth, kills germs, keeps insects away from clothes and grains, gives shade and pure air, protects crops, and is used in medicines, soaps and toothpaste.',
  'Fact sheet – Common name: Neem | Given by: Iranians | Period of birth: millions of years ago | Born at: North India or Myanmar | Found in: Asian and African countries and parts of America | Common names: Nim, Leemba, Nimori, Nimbamond, Nimbe, Nimo, Vepa (Nimb in Hindi) | Names given by scientists: 1. bitter grace of God 2. nature’s gift to man (also cleanliness-parting tree) | Names given by others: 1. magic tree 2. the tree of the twentieth century.']),
 (162, 'Let us discuss (p. 154)', [
  '1. Neem seed powder mixed in water and sprayed keeps locusts away; in rice fields it stops mosquito breeding, saves crops from termites and other insects, and increases crop yield.',
  '2. Cleaning teeth, protecting clothes and stored grains, medicines, soaps and toothpaste, termite-proof furniture, purifying air, stopping mosquitoes.',
  '3. Yes. I used neem leaves in hot bath water when I had itching; my mother keeps neem leaves in the rice drum to keep insects away.']),
 (162, 'Let us think and reflect 1–2', [
  '1. (i) The neem tree was born millions of years ago. (ii) Neem seed powder in stagnant water stops mosquitoes from breeding / furniture made of neem wood is not eaten by termites.',
  '2. a. Neem Baba asked Amber to call him “Neem Baba” because he was very old and she had played in his shade since childhood (it was the tree’s own wish). b. Amber played in the shade of the neem tree (in her courtyard). c. Neem seed powder protects crops from locusts and termites. d. Putting neem seed powder in stagnant water is a cost-effective method to stop mosquitoes from breeding. e. Neem leaves have chemicals that destroy germs, give comfort from itching and do not allow germs to breed.',
  'Note: In the story it is the neem tree (not an old man) that asks Amber to call it Neem Baba.']),
 (163, 'Let us think and reflect 3–13', [
  '3. He was born millions of years ago – it is too long ago to remember an exact year.',
  '4. Leaves, bark, flowers, fruits and even roots.',
  '5. Because it was given many names in different places – Sanskrit names in India, “Neem” by Iranians, many names by scientists – each name tells something about its usefulness; it makes us think.',
  '6. By putting neem seed powder in stagnant water around houses; mosquitoes cannot breed there.',
  '7. Neem twigs clean teeth; neem oil is used in soaps and toothpaste; neem leaves kill germs and stop itching.',
  '8. Health – medicines from leaves, bark, flowers, roots; germ-killing; pure air. Agriculture – keeps away locusts and termites, stops mosquito breeding, increases crop yield, protects stored grains.',
  '9. Because every part of it is useful – for health, cleanliness, farming and the environment – and it costs nothing.',
  '10. Yes. It cures diseases, kills germs, protects crops and grains, keeps mosquitoes away and purifies air – it seems to do magic for us.',
  '11. Wrong amount or wrong use can be harmful; only trained, experienced people know the correct dose and method.',
  '12. Student: Good morning, Neem tree! Why are your leaves bitter? | Neem: Good morning! My bitterness keeps germs and insects away. | Student: Can you help my village? | Neem: Yes, my seeds protect your crops and my leaves keep mosquitoes away. | Student: Thank you! I will water you every day. | Neem: Bless you, child!',
  '13. (As Amber) Today I learnt many wonderful things from Neem Baba. He is millions of years old and is found in many countries. Scientists call him nature’s gift to man. His leaves destroy germs, so they help measles patients. His bark and flowers make medicines, but only trained people should use them. His seed powder protects crops from locusts and termites and stops mosquitoes. Neem oil is used in soaps and toothpaste. I decided to protect trees and discover more qualities of neem when I grow up.']),
 (163, 'Let us learn I (compound words)', [
  'Open compound words: neem oil, well known, seed powder | Closed: something, childhood, grandmother | Hyphenated: well-trained, thought-provoking, cleanliness-parting. More: classroom, sunlight (closed); ice cream, post office (open); mother-in-law, well-being (hyphenated).',
  'Note: the text prints “well trained” and “well known” as two words; used before a noun they are hyphenated (a well-trained doctor).']),
 (164, 'Let us learn II (verbs)', [
  '1–d (became), 2–e (found), 3–a (made), 4–f (discovered), 5–c (told), 6–b (gave). Sentences: I found my lost pen under the desk. / My mother made idlis. / Scientists discovered new uses of neem. / Grandmother told us a story. / My teacher gave me a book.']),
 (165, 'Let us learn III; Let us listen', [
  'III. More personification: The sun smiled at us. The wind howled at night. The stars winked in the sky.',
  'Let us listen (Peepal Baba): Correct statements – c (The mother wanted to know more about Peepal Baba) and d (The boy decided to take care of trees …). a – not correct (it was the boy/teacher who told; the mother had not heard of him); b – wrong (she called it wonderful); e – wrong (he was not worried).']),
 (166, 'Let us write; Let us explore I', [
  'Model paragraph – “My Friend, the Banyan Tree”: A big banyan tree stands by the roadside near our school at T.V. Palli. It is very tall with a thick trunk and many hanging roots. Its leaves are broad and green, and it has small red fruits. Birds and squirrels live on it. I like it because it gives cool shade to travellers and farmers, and we play under it during lunch break. Its roots and leaves are also used as medicine.',
  'Explore I: Nimb – Hindi; Nim – Bengali; Leemba – Gujarati/Marathi (Limdo, Limb); Vepa – Telugu (Veppam – Tamil). For Nimori, Nimbamond, Nimbe, Nimo the book asks you to find out with your teacher (regional/dialect names).']),
 (167, 'Let us explore II – Green Passport', [
  'Steps: 1. Choose a spot and plant a sapling (e.g., neem). 2. Record the plant’s name, date of planting and growth every week. 3. Water and care for it regularly; note what it needs. 4. Avoid plastic; recycle and compost. 5. Share your experience in the school assembly on SASA Day and invite classmates to join.'])]
